     5	
     6	## Purpose
     7	
     8	Maintain a thesis library: create theses, update their judgements and close them when due. Upstream continuously supplies classified information. Extraction reads each whole item as a senior hedge-fund analyst, alongside existing theses, and returns linked evidence and candidates with research-group associations, declaring new groups where needed. The library preserves those associations and compares identity: merge, succession, new thesis or skip; repeat, replace or new evidence. Each thesis has a question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due.
     9	
    10	D149 requires organisation during full-text reading, with associations included in extraction output. See [the library's grouping contract](06-views.md#research-groups-associate-during-extraction) and [extraction's responsibility](05-extraction.md#determine-associations-during-extraction). Rubin shipments and SK factory deployment are separate questions in one continuing business process. Default integration is pending; the [improvement plan](../poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md) records implementation gaps, D155 identity changes and validation work.
    11	
    12	## Why rebuild
    13	
    14	The old system pulled dated batches. Category definitions existed in nine modules, configuration used five overriding layers, and time had several clocks. Explaining an item's path required reading multiple implementations. The rebuild gives each decision one owner.
    15	
    16	## System boundary
    17	
    18	- **Upstream, outside this system:** acquire material, classify it, identify related subjects and publication time, and deliver it. Acquisition details are deferred.
    19	- **This system:** dispatch through the library, final verdict and pull mode.
    20	- **Tests:** stream stored items in publication order through the product's path, with replay time supplied to pull mode.
    21	
    22	```text
    23	Upstream → Dispatch → Extraction → Thesis library ↔ Matching labels
    24	                         │               ↑
    25	                  read-only; groups   Pull mode: scheduled review,
    26	                                      freeze and close due theses
    27	Final verdict: earlier draft; decide its role with pull mode
    28	```
    29	
    30	| From | To | Content |
    31	|---|---|---|
    32	| Research universe | Upstream | Subjects and tracked categories |
    33	| Research universe | Extraction | All themes for candidate selection in block 3 |
    34	| Config | Extraction, library, final verdict | Model settings version; library search result count |
    35	| Library | Extraction | Open subject theses and their dependencies: question, focus, due date and links; available groups under the library contract, in block 5 |
    36	| Library | Matching labels | Comparison records for human labels and future smaller-model training |
    37	| Dispatch, extraction, library | Human review | Unresolved items, proposed themes and repeated failures |
    38	
    39	## Two modes
    40	
    41	| | Incoming stream | Pull mode |
    42	|---|---|---|
    43	| Trigger | An item arrives | Day-end or scheduled interval |
    44	| Initiator | Upstream delivery | System scheduling |
    45	| Work | Read evidence/candidates and associations, preserve associations while matching identity, rejudge changed inputs under library rules | Overall review/update; freeze and close due theses; remaining design deferred |
    46	| Calendar | Never reads it | Sole time-driven module; receives “now” explicitly |
    47	
    48	## Principles
    49	
    50	1. Start from theses. Their needs determine what to read and retain; do not build a financial-number warehouse first.
    51	2. A thesis is a judgement; evidence records who said what in which item. Only incoming information creates evidence. Theses cannot prove each other.
    52	3. Retain sources for every thesis, evidence item and information-triggered change, including the location in the original.
    53	4. The stream is driven by incoming information, not the calendar. No input means no stream work; pull mode owns time.
    54	5. Dispatch sequence determines order. Read earlier sequences plus the current round's own writes, with results equivalent to serial processing. Append knowledge without rewriting history. Log obsolete late statements; accept newly learned matters with their actual dates.
    55	6. The research universe alone chooses subjects and categories. Upstream supplies only that scope; process every supplied item.
    56	7. At matching time, open one thesis per subject and focus, with separate current/next-year numeric periods; manual reopening is excluded. D155 governs the POC's bounded code-owned identity. Search finds comparisons, not final identity. Resolve an evidence item's thesis before searching within it.
    57	8. Fix questions and ladders in advance, and freeze the state when due. Rungs are verifiable outcomes. Append records and retain closed theses and replaced evidence.
    58	9. Do not guess unresolved identifiers or rules; use human review.
    59	10. Do not copy whole source material into the library. Retrieve it from the team's source store by ID.
    60	
    61	## Glossary
    62	
    63	Use one term per concept in the maintained design. `docs/check.py` checks terminology in the relevant page data and PRDs. Historical quotations, old source records and identifiers keep their original text. English replaces the previous Chinese presentation under D163; it does not rename stored identities.
    64	
    65	| Term | Meaning | Avoid |
    66	|---|---|---|
