    68	| Item | One upstream unit processed by classification, dispatch and extraction | Using file for every kind of item |
    69	| File | Text-form source such as earnings, a report or news; qualify filesystem files as data/configuration files | Confusing source form with category |
    70	| Table data | Fixed-field information; one release, such as a 13F or statistical release, is one item | Treating every cell as an item |
    71	| Source material | Raw upstream material before classification | Calling excluded material an invalid item automatically |
    72	| Form | Text or table | Using it for genre |
    73	| Genre | Transcript, notes, memo, report, slides, article, letter or post | — |
    74	| Filing | Formal submission to a regulator, exchange or court; SEC filing where applicable | SEC document as a category |
    75	| Filing code | 10-K, 8-K, DEF 14A, etc. | Table code |
    76	| Category | One of T1–T16 | Category type |
    77	| Classification result | A category, duplicate merge, not source material, or invalid | Assuming every item has a category |
    78	| Metadata | Supplied classification result, form, subjects, publication time and other attributes | — |
    79	| Related subject | Company, cryptocurrency, commodity or theme identified for the item; it need not be in our universe | Equating company with stock |
    80	| Subject identifier | Typed CIK, ticker, registered company name, cryptocurrency/commodity symbol or theme ID | One untyped subject code |
    81	| Author | The party responsible for content; distinguish named individual and speaker | Treating delivery channel as author |
    82	| Author type | Company, media, analyst, official or individual; author table then channel default | Category type |
    83	| Publication time / publication day | Public-release timestamp / its New York date | Receipt time as publication day |
    84	| Upstream ID / sequence number | Stable upstream version identity / dispatch-owned arrival order | Dispatch serial number |
    85	| Original item | The item a duplicate merges into | — |
    86	| Invalid | A classification result with a reason | Mixing it with excluded source material |
    87	| Human review pool | Unresolved matters awaiting a person | Unqualified review pool |
    88	| Dataset / data interface | Registered collection of table data / access channel | Confusing either with category |
    89	| Prompt template / prompt / upload template | Shared five-block structure / assembled category instructions / upload table format | Unqualified template |
    90	| Role | Senior hedge-fund analyst in prompt block 1 | Identity, which names a record or subject |
    91	| Thesis / candidate thesis | An immutable question, ladder and due date / extraction's proposed uncovered question, including personal-memo judgements | Calling every quoted opinion a thesis; naked candidate where ambiguous |
    92	| Opinion | An author's, analyst's or media commentator's view | Confusing it with a stored thesis |
    93	| Statement | What a source party says | — |
    94	| Asset thesis / theme thesis | Tradable subject with business-impact rungs / theme with outcome-labelled rungs | Company view, object view, theme view |
    95	| Focus | The quantity and year or event the thesis concerns, alongside its subject | Thesis driver |
    96	| Ladder / rung | Exhaustive, non-overlapping outcomes: five, or two for yes/no | Fixed five-rung ladder; old verification-condition design |
    97	| Due date | When the debate can resolve; a number's publication day | Expiry bucket |
    98	| State | Our current rung judgement; not yet judged before a memo thesis first receives evidence | A second parallel state |
    99	| Conclusion | State plus reasons; may change through key facts/numbers even within one rung | Equating changed conclusion with changed score only |
   100	| Freeze | Preserve the current state and reasons at the due date, then close | A separate confirmed/falsified verdict in the current design |
   101	| Unavailable answer / pending publication | Proposed pull-mode cases, not yet approved | Treating proposals as implemented states |
   102	| Evidence tier | Fact, party statement, third-party view or rumour; only facts can establish the outcome | Mixing tiers with thesis kinds |
   103	| Link | Evidence linked to a thesis, with its judgement-specific push | Treating push as intrinsic to evidence |
   104	| Search / comparison list | Find plausible matches / top N shown to matching | Treating search ranking as identity |
   105	| Open / closed | The lifecycle switch; only open theses update; closed theses remain searchable | Active thesis, inactive thesis |
   106	| Repeat / replace / new evidence | Same existing statement / a later statement superseding it / another event, period or speaker | Counting repeated references as independent evidence |
   107	| Replaced | Historical evidence no longer in force, kept for traceability | Deleted evidence |
   108	| Dependency | A thesis reads another's conclusion with a specified direction; no cycles | Premise in the current design |
   109	| Succession / chain | Next period of the same focus / its ordered sequence; evidence replacement also forms chains | Confusing succession with dependency |
   110	| Matching / matching record | Decide destinations and retain inputs, answers, checks and writes | Inferring identity solely from similar wording |
   111	| Merge | A candidate joins an open thesis or a part of it, inheriting its ladder and due date | Rewriting the target's identity |
   112	| Source reference | Upstream ID, category, publication day, location and quote/cell | — |
   113	| Original source | Who first said it and when; used for independence and replacement ordering | Counting each retelling independently |
   114	| Standalone summary | Evidence readable without its source; used by matching and rejudgement | — |
   115	| Duplicate check | Proposed scheduled consolidation of duplicate open theses; not yet approved | Treating it as implemented |
   116	| Rejudgement / round | Recompute from evidence in force and dependencies / all changes from one item | Incremental score arithmetic |
   117	| Matching labels / smaller model | Human-labelled destinations / future faster model trained from them | Assuming labels already provide acceptance |
   118	| Code | Programmed rules, distinct from model judgement | — |
   119	| Family / group | Five classification families / twelve theme-library groups | Calling theme groups categories |
   120	| Frozen design | A decided module version; changes need a new decision | Confusing design freeze with a frozen thesis state |
