     1	# Extraction
     2	
     3	> Status: foundation frozen, October 18, 2026; category prompt wording needs validation. D149 requires extraction-time grouping, not yet integrated into the default pipeline. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).
     4	> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Extraction → Details. Recorded decisions take precedence over this PRD.
     5	
     6	## Purpose
     7	
     8	Read each whole item with its category prompt as a senior hedge-fund analyst. Return evidence linked to shown or candidate theses, and candidates for important uncovered questions. Each candidate also carries structured group associations and any necessary new group definitions. Every output retains its source. Hand everything to the library, which owns the [grouping contract](06-views.md#research-groups-associate-during-extraction). Personal memos follow the prompt rule below.
     9	
    10	## Rationale
    11	
    12	- Extraction reads; the library compares and stores. Those responsibilities remain separate.
    13	- Read the complete file with embedded tables, or an entire table release, without separate text/table pipelines.
    14	- Categories require different reading: news for events and attribution, research for views and ratings, official decisions for actions and effective dates. They share one prompt structure.
    15	- A thesis is the analyst's judgement expressed as a question, ladder and due date. Evidence is an objective record of who said what in which item. Most items add evidence; fewer create new questions.
    16	- Evidence matters in relation to a question. Block 5 supplies existing subject theses and their dependencies, allowing a company statement about assured export licences to link directly to the licence theme.
    17	- Important uncovered matters, including another named company, a new material issue or a macro/policy event, become candidates with initiating evidence. The library later determines whether they are existing or new questions.
    18	- Sources make both theses and evidence traceable to the original text.
    19	
    20	## One item, step by step
    21	
    22	The model performs step 2; code performs the others.
    23	
    24	<!-- generated: steps -->
    25	```
    26	An item arrives with category and sequence number
    27	  ↓ 1 Build the prompt (Code): Use the category's content in the shared template. The library supplies block 5: open subject theses, their dependencies and existing groups needed for association.
    28	  ↓ 2 Read (Model): Read the whole item. Return evidence linked to block-5 theses or current candidates, and candidates for important uncovered questions with their initiating evidence. Personal memos follow their special rule. Decide each candidate's group association and return references under the library contract. Retain sources.
    29	  ↓ 3 Check sources and group references (Code): Check that the quote or cell exists in the item and that cited numbers appear there. Evidence must link to block-5 theses or current candidates. Reject source mismatches with reasons. Validate association references under the library's complete handoff contract.
    30	  ↓ 4 Hand off to the library (Code): Hand off evidence, candidates, associations and necessary group definitions with the sequence number. Matching decides identity; writing preserves membership.
    31	```
    32	<!-- /generated: steps -->
    33	
    34	## Two outputs: evidence and candidates
    35	
    36	One reading returns evidence, candidates, group associations and necessary new group definitions. Evidence may link to shown theses or current candidates. Candidates are reserved for important uncovered questions, subject to the personal-memo rule. The library first resolves a candidate's destination, then compares its evidence within that thesis.
    37	
    38	<!-- generated: two -->
    39	|  | Evidence | Candidate thesis |
    40	|---|---|---|
    41	| What it is | A statement or number from the item: who said what or what happened | A complete question to track: subject, question, ladder, due date and research-group association |
    42	| How common | Present in almost every item | Few: important questions uncovered by block 5; also all judgements from the user's memo |
    43	| Links | One or more block-5 theses, or current candidates | Carries initiating evidence; candidates from the user's memo may omit it |
    44	| In the library | Compare against its thesis's existing evidence: repeat, replacement or new | Decide existing identity or a part of it, succession, new thesis or skip. Preserve associations with the final thesis; matching does not regroup. |
    45	| Example | The 8-K statement that H20 exports to China require a licence | What will the China export-licence regime for chips covered by the April 2025 rule be on April 15, 2026? |
    46	
    47	```
    48	Read a statement or number | Which thesis does it concern? (Judgements in the user's memo bypass this tree: all become candidates and may lack evidence)
    49	├─ A thesis in block 5 → Evidence: link to that thesis (Link only to the thesis directly discussed, not also to theses that depend on it)
    50	├─ Not in block 5, but important → Candidate thesis with this statement as evidence (Return its group association during extraction. The library later decides existing identity or new thesis. A thesis covers only up to its due date; events after that date belong here too.)
    51	└─ Neither → Do not extract (Background or boilerplate)
    52	```
    53	<!-- /generated: two -->
    54	
    55	## Prompt template
    56	
    57	All categories use these five blocks. Classification owns the category's definition and reading method.
    58	
    59	<!-- generated: template -->
    60	| Part | Writes | Source |
    61	|---|---|---|
    62	| Role | You are a senior hedge-fund analyst. Read this item to test and form theses: link supporting or opposing statements and numbers as evidence to existing theses. For important questions not covered, return complete candidates with a question, ladder, due date, research-group association and initiating evidence. Judgements in the user's own memo become candidates, may lack evidence and are not themselves evidence. Each thesis concerns one thing. Its ladder has five rungs, or two for yes/no, with concrete, verifiable, non-overlapping and exhaustive outcomes, never vague “partial” outcomes. Set the due date when the issue can be resolved; for a number, use its publication date. Name the year. This year and next year are relative to the item's publication day; company numbers use its fiscal year. | Shared by all categories; thesis rules come directly from the library |
    63	| What this category is and how to read it | The category definition and reading method; for example, attribute opinions and estimates to their author rather than treating them as completed facts. | Owned by classification; not redefined here |
    64	| Subjects | The item's related subjects plus every theme in the research universe. Select an existing theme for a theme candidate when applicable; otherwise describe the new issue clearly. | Item metadata; themes from the research universe |
    65	| What to extract from this category | One row per category in the table below. | Extraction module |
    66	| Existing theses and research groups | Open theses for the item's subjects and their dependencies, with dependency links. Supply only question, focus and due date, not states or evidence. Also supply available research groups under the library's grouping contract. | Thesis library |
    67	<!-- /generated: template -->
    68	
    69	## Category-specific prompts
    70	
    71	T7 news illustrates how the five blocks fit together.
    72	
    73	<!-- generated: prompt -->
    74	```
    75	T7 news prompt (The same template, with news-specific blocks 2 and 4)
    76	├─ 1 Role → Senior hedge-fund analyst (Read to test and form theses. Link evidence to existing theses; for important uncovered questions, return complete candidates with question, ladder, due date and research-group association.)
    77	├─ 2 How to read this category → Media reporting (From classification: opinion and analysis belong to T8; registered publication series use their category; remaining media material belongs here.)
    78	├─ 3 Subjects → Subjects of this report (Filled by code, such as NVDA and AMD, plus all universe themes)
    79	├─ 4 What to extract → What happened, when and who was involved; attribution and original sources (Official confirmation is fact; unnamed-source claims are rumours)
    80	└─ 5 Existing theses and research groups → Open theses for the report's subjects and their dependencies (Filled by code with dependency links, questions, focus and due dates. For example, NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 and its chip-export-licence dependency. Also include available research groups.)
    81	```
    82	
    83	- Each category has its own prompt using the same template. Blocks 2 and 4 vary by category; block 1 is shared; code fills blocks 3 and 5 for the item.
    84	- One prompt per tracked category T1–T15. T16 is not tracked by default; write its prompt when needed.
    85	- For theme candidates, select from block 3 or describe a new issue if absent. For companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities, provide the relevant symbol or name. Code resolves reference data and accepts registered names only, as classification does.
    86	- The user's own memo (T8 authored by the user) yields candidate judgements, not evidence. These candidates may have no evidence. Once opened, their state is “not yet judged” until evidence first links to them.
    87	- Code defines and validates output fields for evidence, candidates and associations, necessary new groups and sources. Do not restate the shape in prompts.
    88	- Test each category's prompt on stored items before settling its wording. Config records each version; a change creates a new version.
    89	<!-- /generated: prompt -->
