     1	# Thesis library
     2	
     3	> Status: foundation frozen, October 20, 2026. D149 adds extraction-owned grouping; default integration remains pending. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163). Only recorded decisions apply; open questions and two pull-mode proposals remain separate.
     4	> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Thesis library → Details. This PRD also owns Matching labels. Recorded decisions take precedence. Historical discussion: `docs/views-rebuild-log.md`.
     5	
     6	## Purpose
     7	
     8	A thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare evidence within its thesis to repeat, replace or add. Extra source references alone do not trigger rejudgement. Extraction determines group associations; the writer preserves them after resolving final thesis IDs under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction).
     9	
    10	## Rationale
    11	
    12	- Clear structures connect theses, ladders and evidence to source items.
    13	- A thesis is a question with a state, not an assertion. Opposing views move the same state's direction rather than creating opposite duplicate theses.
    14	- A fixed, verifiable ladder states in advance what would disprove the view, analogous to explicit analyst scenarios or predefined investment exit conditions. Do not change it after observing the outcome.
    15	- The model judges from all evidence still in force and dependency conclusions, at low temperature, rather than adjusting its previous score. Replacement follows statement date, so late old statements do not restore obsolete evidence.
    16	- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.
    17	- A thesis contains one period and one due date, generally 6–12 months; intermediate quarterly reports are evidence.
    18	- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.
    19	- Evidence needs the same search-then-compare discipline. Otherwise February's 12% capex guidance and April's 25% revision remain side by side, forcing every judgement to rediscover which is current.
    20	- Record repeat/replacement decisions once. Keep replaced evidence in history. A retelling alone should not require another model judgement to establish that nothing changed.
    21	- Use two rungs for yes/no instead of forcing five.
    22	- The model chooses dependencies like an analyst; code rejects cycles. Apparently mutual dependencies indicate poorly defined questions.
    23	- Rejudge when inputs change. A changed conclusion includes a new rung or materially changed supporting facts/numbers. A repeated Fed statement should not trigger hundreds of dependants; AMZN capex moving from 12% to 25% can trigger NVDA even within the same rung.
    24	- Start matching with a general-purpose model, retain records, obtain human labels and later train a faster smaller model.
    25	
    26	## Structure
    27	
    28	<!-- generated: structure -->
    29	```
    30	Thesis library
    31	├─ Subject → Companies, cryptocurrencies, commodities and themes in the universe
    32	│   └─ Thesis → A question + a ladder + a due date (Immutable once written. Five rungs, or two for yes/no. When matching opens a thesis, only one may be open for the same subject and focus at a time.)
    33	│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model judges from facts. A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)
    34	│       ├─ Link → Evidence (Store evidence once with sources and tier. Its push belongs to the link. Replaced evidence remains in history and no longer counts.)
    35	│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)
    36	│       └─ Succession → The previous period's thesis (Successive periods of the same focus form a chain)
    37	├─ Research group → Organise theses by a continuing business process (Associate during extraction; full rules are in “Research groups: associate during extraction”)
    38	└─ Append-only records → Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labelling records (Append at every step, without editing or deleting. Read records up to a historical date to recover its judgement.)
    39	```
    40	<!-- /generated: structure -->
    41	
    42	## Two thesis kinds
    43	
    44	<!-- generated: kinds -->
    45	| Kind | Subject kind | Ladder rungs | Example |
    46	|---|---|---|---|
    47	| Asset thesis | Tradable subjects: companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities | Five rungs: strongly bullish, bullish, neutral, bearish, strongly bearish. Two for yes/no: bullish and bearish. These describe the business impact; set them at creation and keep them fixed. | How will NVDA FY2027 China revenue compare with FY2026? |
    48	| Theme thesis | Macro, policy and industry themes | Ordered outcomes from one end to the other: five rungs, or two for yes/no. Name outcomes without judging them good or bad. Dependent asset theses specify which outcome benefits them. | How will the Fed's target-rate upper bound after its last 2026 meeting compare with now? (Large cut … large hike) |
    49	
    50	The same event can affect assets differently: rate cuts can be bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM net interest income. Theme theses therefore do not label outcomes good or bad.
    51	<!-- /generated: kinds -->
    52	
    53	## Ladder
    54	
    55	<!-- generated: ladder -->
    56	```
    57	Two kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)
    58	├─ Asset thesis: numeric → NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 | Five rungs describing NVDA's business impact (Due on the FY2027 annual earnings date)
    59	│   ├─ Strongly bullish → Up at least 50%
    60	│   ├─ Bullish → Up at least 10% but under 50%
    61	│   ├─ Neutral → Up or down by less than 10%
    62	│   ├─ Bearish → Down at least 10% but under 80%
    63	│   └─ Strongly bearish → Down at least 80% (At most one fifth remains)
    64	└─ Theme thesis: event → China export licensing for chips covered by the April 2025 rule, including H20 and MI308, as of April 15, 2026 | Five ordered outcomes, without good/bad labels (Use the rules in force on that date)
    65	    ├─ Rung 1 → Full ban; licence applications no longer accepted
    66	    ├─ Rung 2 → Licences required; none issued
    67	    ├─ Rung 3 → Licences required and issued; some conditional
    68	    ├─ Rung 4 → Licences required and issued; all unconditional
    69	    └─ Rung 5 → Licences no longer required
    70	```
    71	
    72	1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed.
    73	2. Each rung is verifiable: a numeric interval or event stage. Rungs must be exhaustive and non-overlapping, so exactly one applies at expiry. For example: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Every boundary belongs to one rung. “Up a lot/a little” cannot be checked. Avoid vague partial outcomes; specify the partial state, such as conditional licences, as a distinct rung. State the comparison underlying neutral.
    74	3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.
    75	4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.
    76	5. Numeric theses name a year. Non-company quantities use calendar years: the publication year is this year and the following year is next year, each ending December 31. Company quantities use the fiscal year containing publication day and its successor. Never use the computer clock. For a given quantity, open at most one thesis for each of this year and next year; do not open later years. The due date is the quantity's publication date.
    77	6. Set the due date when the debate can be resolved, usually 6–12 months away. Intermediate quarterly reports are evidence, not separate theses.
    78	<!-- /generated: ladder -->
    79	
    80	## State
    81	
    82	<!-- generated: positions -->
    83	|  | Meaning | Decision maker | Example |
    84	|---|---|---|---|
    85	| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges at low temperature from facts. Reconsider all evidence still in force, including each item's replaced predecessor to show the change, plus current dependency states and reasons. Do not increment or decrement the previous score. Cite evidence and compare with the conclusion before this round to state whether it changed. Do not rejudge if only source references were added and dependency conclusions did not change. | After the 8-K: strongly bearish |
    86	
    87	- Every judgement states its basis in a fixed response shape: evidence IDs such as e12 and e15, plus dependency thesis IDs such as #1 when their changes triggered rejudgement.
    88	- Code checks cited IDs against records: evidence must belong to this thesis and not be replaced; cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then human review after repeated failure. This checks references, not whether the judgement is correct, like verifying a cited page exists.
    89	- Each judgement also declares whether the conclusion changed and explains why, comparing with the state and reasons before this round, including during retries. That earlier conclusion is for comparison, not incremental scoring. A rung change or a material change in the supporting facts/numbers counts: AMZN capex guidance moving from 12% to 25% growth changes the conclusion even within one rung. Repetition does not. A changed conclusion triggers dependent theses in the same round. Code requires changed=true for a first judgement or rung change; otherwise refuse under the same correction rules.
    90	- For historical judgement, read the records saved on that date. Do not ask the model again; a new sample can differ.
    91	<!-- /generated: positions -->
    92	
    93	## Four evidence tiers
    94	
    95	<!-- generated: tiers -->
    96	| Tier | Example | Role |
    97	|---|---|---|
    98	| Fact | Official actions, published figures and completed transactions | Can establish which rung holds and move the state |
    99	| Party statement | Company guidance, management statements and official speeches | Can move the state only |
   100	| Third-party view | Sell-side analysts, media analysis and experts | Can move the state only |
   101	| Rumour | Unnamed-source claims and unattributed statements | Can move the state only |
   102	
   103	- The model determines the size of the push and gives reasons. No tier has a fixed score.
   104	- Count independent original sources. Twenty outlets retelling one exclusive are twenty references to one evidence item, not twenty independent confirmations.
   105	- Tier follows who confirmed it. An unnamed-source report that Commerce is issuing licences is a rumour. An official or company confirmation is factual evidence replacing it. The rumour remains a rumour in history.
   106	- Store evidence once without an intrinsic direction. Each rejudgement writes its push on the evidence–thesis link. One statement can affect multiple theses differently.
   107	<!-- /generated: tiers -->
   108	
   109	## Dependencies: reading the arrows
   110	
   111	<!-- generated: dependencies -->
   112	```
   113	B depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)
   114	├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)
   115	│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons
   116	│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)
   117	│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)
   118	└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)
   119	    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons
   120	    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)
   121	    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”
   122	```
   123	
   124	- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.
   125	- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.
   126	- Dependencies are the sole way theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.
   127	- Count a statement once. A statement about A is A's evidence, including when two candidates arise from one item. B learns it through A's conclusion. Only statements directly about B are B's evidence. If B does not depend on A, the statement may also directly evidence B. Choose dependencies only when B is created.
   128	- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, or a dependency declaring a changed conclusion. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Closed theses do not update. A memo-derived thesis is first judged when evidence first links to it; before that, it and its dependants see “not yet judged”.
   129	- The matching model chooses dependencies when opening a thesis, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction. Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.
   130	- Before adding an edge or following it to a successor, code checks for cycles. Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling. Apparently mutually dependent questions indicate poorly defined theses.
   131	<!-- /generated: dependencies -->
   132	
   133	## Succession: the same focus over successive periods
   134	
   135	<!-- generated: chain -->
   136	- Successive periods of the same focus form a chain, such as Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 rate → 2028 rate. The chain records period order. Dependency traversal follows the rules above.
   137	- One thesis has at most one successor: FY2026 cannot produce two FY2027 successors.
   138	- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.
   139	<!-- /generated: chain -->
   140	
   141	## Intake: search then compare
   142	
   143	<!-- generated: shape -->
   144	|  | Candidate thesis | Evidence |
   145	|---|---|---|
   146	| Comparison set | Top N search results from the entire thesis library | Top N evidence results within its thesis |
   147	| Decision | Existing identity or part, succession, new thesis or skip | Repeat, replace or new evidence |
   148	| Chain | Succession: Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 → 2028 | Replacement: AMZN capex guidance 12% growth → 25% |
   149	| What counts as current | Open theses on the chain; numeric theses may have one for this year and one for next year | Evidence not replaced; replacements also form a chain |
   150	| Search scope | Entire thesis library, open and closed | Only evidence within its thesis, not the entire library |
   151	<!-- /generated: shape -->
   152	
   153	## One item entering the library
   154	
   155	Process one round per item in sequence order. Candidates arrive with associations and necessary group definitions, preserved under the grouping rules. The model performs steps 4 and 7; code performs the others.
   156	
   157	<!-- generated: intake -->
   158	```
   159	Evidence and candidates from extraction: one round per item
   160	  ↓ 1 Check sources (Code): Reject sources absent from processing records and record the reason
   161	  ↓ 2 Receive evidence (Code): Retries do not duplicate evidence. A revised input updates only its own source reference. A revised retelling does not alter the original evidence. If the revision is from the original source, compare its statements normally and replace changed statements.
   162	  ↓ 3 Search (Code): Use a search engine such as Elasticsearch; semantic versus keyword search remains open. Search all open and closed theses for each candidate and take top N, initially 10 in Config. Search evidence only within its linked thesis, also top N. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, even if also linked to existing theses. Empty lists still proceed to matching.
   163	  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The model does not read the original item. Show each existing thesis's subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state. In one call: (1) decide candidate identity or a narrower part, succession with unchanged ladder orientation but new thresholds, new thesis for distinct or merely similar questions, or skip overly broad questions, numbers beyond this/next year and the same period of a closed thesis; give reasons; (2) choose dependencies and directions among searched, block-5 and same-item new theses; (3) compare evidence linked only to existing theses. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, then searches all linked existing theses together and uses one further comparison call. Evidence linked only to new theses is new evidence.
   164	  ↓ 5 Validate (Code): Destination IDs must be in the shown lists or the relevant thesis's evidence. Merge only into open theses. New subjects must resolve within the universe using classification's identifier rules; otherwise skip. Dependencies must reference shown theses or an explicit awaited chain/year and remain acyclic. Return invalid answers for correction.
   165	  ↓ 6 Write to the library (Code): Resolve extraction's group associations to final theses and save them in this step. Each subject has its own writing queue; write theme theses before asset theses. Save new theses with their dependency edges. A repeat adds a source reference and any new thesis link. For replacement, order by when the statement was made: later statements supersede earlier ones. A late-arriving older statement is logged, not inserted. Carry the old evidence's other thesis links to its replacement. An original-source revision that omits a statement without replacing it marks it replaced. If compared data changes before writing, repeat matching only for the affected part.
   166	  ↓ 7 Run a rejudgement round (Model): Rejudge only open theses whose inputs changed: new/replaced/newly linked evidence, or a changed dependency conclusion, newly created awaited thesis or successor. Judge dependencies first and each thesis once per round. Extra source references alone do not trigger it. Memo-derived theses without evidence stay not yet judged. Each judgement declares whether its conclusion changed; changed conclusions propagate to dependants.
   167	```
   168	<!-- /generated: intake -->
   169	
   170	## Step 4: candidate destinations
   171	
   172	<!-- generated: outcomes -->
   173	| Destination | When | Writes |
   174	|---|---|---|
   175	| Merge into existing | The candidate concerns the same focus or a part of it, such as H20 China revenue within China revenue. Merge only into open theses; closed theses can only be succeeded. | Compare its evidence within the target thesis and preserve grouping under this section's rules. Use the existing ladder and due date. |
   176	| Succession | The next period of the same focus: the next year for numbers, or continued tracking after an event thesis closed | Create a thesis and record succession |
   177	| New thesis | Distinct or merely similar: Blackwell export licences and H20 export licences are different questions | Create a thesis with its ladder and due date fixed from now on |
   178	| Skip | Too broad or mixed, bundling questions that can move oppositely, such as all US chip controls on China; numbers beyond this/next year; the same focus and period as a closed thesis; or an out-of-universe subject. Proposed themes go to human review; out-of-universe companies receive only a processing-log entry. | Do not create it. Evidence also linked to a specific valid thesis still counts; evidence linked only to the skipped candidate is not stored. |
   179	
   180	- The D155 POC uses code for fully determined duplicate identity and model comparison when scope or legacy identity needs judgement. Reference lists guide research without vetoing candidates. Same group does not mean same thesis. See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for the contract and measured scope.
   181	- Search finds plausible candidates; the model handles semantic comparison. H20 and Blackwell export licences can look similar but are separate questions. Bullish and bearish accounts of one question share its identity.
   182	- Search includes closed theses, allowing an FY2027 candidate to find closed FY2026 and be classified as succession.
   183	<!-- /generated: outcomes -->
   184	
   185	## Step 4: evidence destinations
   186	
   187	<!-- generated: evidence-outcomes -->
   188	| Destination | When | Writes |
   189	|---|---|---|
   190	| Repeat | The same evidence already exists under the thesis: a retelling, the same number in text and table, or the same speaker repeating the same statement | Add a source reference, not another evidence item. Existing links do not trigger rejudgement; a new thesis link changes that thesis's inputs and does. |
   191	| Replace | A later account of the same matter: a speaker revises guidance, forecast or event progress; an official confirms/denies a rumour; or the original source revises its statement | Order by statement date. Later statements replace earlier ones, which remain in history; rejudge. A late-arriving older statement is only logged and does not trigger rejudgement. |
   192	| New evidence | Anything else: a new event, another period's figure or another speaker's statement, including disagreement. Uncertainty also means new evidence. | Add it alongside existing evidence and rejudge |
   193	
   194	- First resolve the evidence's thesis destination from extraction or candidate matching. Search the top N evidence items still in force within that thesis and ask the model to compare. Unlike candidate-thesis search, evidence search never spans the whole library.
   195	- Store evidence once even when linked to several theses. Compare all those theses' evidence together. If any link is to a candidate, wait for that candidate's destination.
   196	- Carry other thesis links from replaced evidence to the replacement and rejudge open linked theses; otherwise they would retain only an obsolete statement.
   197	- Replacement applies to a statement or number, not the whole report. A revised sell-side revenue forecast replaces that forecast while other arguments remain in force.
   198	- Matching reads the evidence summary, speaker, original source and statement date, not the full original item. When uncertain, treat it as new evidence; both then remain available for judgement.
   199	<!-- /generated: evidence-outcomes -->
