   391	## Research groups: associate during extraction
   392	
   393	<!-- generated: relations -->
   394	- D149 requirement: organise theses by the continuing business process they discuss, called a research group. Vera Rubin readiness and customer deployment can share a group while shipment dates, SK factory launch and OpenAI deployment retain separate questions, ladders and due dates. A group may start with one thesis; a thesis may belong to several groups. A group has no state or ladder, creates no automatic dependency and is not an overarching investment thesis for an agent.
   395	- Associate by business process, across companies and industries, not merely by company. A customer deployment explicitly using Rubin can join Rubin; NVIDIA PC chips cannot join just because they share a company. OpenAI deployment cannot join an SK-specific group merely because both involve Rubin. A group does not replace a research-universe theme.
   396	- Before the full-text call, code supplies available group IDs, names and scopes. Search is not restricted by company. During that call, extraction references shown groups or declares new named and scoped groups. Every candidate needs nonempty, unique references, each shown or declared. Code validates the complete references before writing. Declare a new group if none fits; do not force an approximate fit.
   397	- Matching still decides thesis/evidence identity and dependencies; it does not reread association fields to regroup. After creation, succession or merging, the writer resolves candidate IDs to final thesis IDs and local group IDs to stored group IDs, then stores membership. Candidates failing source checks or ultimately skipped do not become members. Do not create empty groups for unused declarations or reject other valid memberships because of them.
   398	- Store each group/member pair once, with input ID and sequence number under the library's ordering and historical-read rules. Commit groups and memberships atomically with theses and evidence; roll all back on failure. Never mark a partial write complete. Redelivery must not duplicate membership; use existing dispatch and recovery.
   399	- Groups do not copy evidence. Read sources through members' real evidence links, filtered by the requested sequence and excluding evidence already replaced at that point. No link means no evidence. A new member or an existing thesis change flag updates its groups. A repeated quote only adds a source and does not trigger an update. Evidence-only articles update existing memberships without inventing theses or groups.
   400	- Acceptance measures source-supported membership and evidence retention, not identical names, IDs or group counts on every run. Report omissions, check refusals, missed associations and wrong associations separately. Preserve Rubin shipment/customer-deployment relationships and check wrong links to unrelated products or customer-specific groups. Multi-company and large-library behaviour is not yet validated.
   401	- Implementation reference remains an isolated experiment: D147 supplies existing_subjects; threads declares id, name and scope; candidates[].thread_ids holds references. Local IDs are t plus a positive integer; stored IDs are g plus a positive integer. Prototype search uses title/full-text words against a global name/scope index, taking N from existing Config. These are prototype fields and search choices, not default-pipeline capabilities or evidence of large-library recall.
   402	- See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for gaps and steps. D148 member context and D150 reason-first output did not solve wrong grouping. D152's four name-selection responses to the third article created no candidates, so new membership was unexercised. Preserve all prior responses and results. Overarching thesis generation, MCP presentation and historical backfill are outside that implementation scope.
