   120	What two items yield (Illustration)
   121	├─ NVIDIA 8-K, April 15, 2025 → Empty library; no theses in block 5 | Two candidate theses (T6 other company publication: H20 exports to China require a licence; expected charges up to approximately $5.5 billion)
   122	│   ├─ Candidate: theme thesis → What will China export licensing for chips covered by the April 2025 rule, including H20 and MI308, look like on April 15, 2026? (Event thesis. Five rungs: full ban; licences required and none issued; licences issued with some conditions; all issued licences unconditional; no licence required. Due April 15, 2026. Quote: “…requires a license for export to China … of the Company’s H20 integrated circuits…”. NVIDIA discloses the licence requirement, classified as fact. The library opens thesis #1.)
   123	│   └─ Candidate: NVDA asset thesis → How will NVDA FY2026 China revenue compare with FY2025? (Numeric thesis. Five rungs: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Due on the FY2026 annual earnings date. Quote: “…up to approximately $5.5 billion of charges…”. This is a company expectation, classified as a party statement. The library opens #2, dependent on #1.)
   124	└─ NVIDIA statement, July 14, 2025 → Block 5 contains #2 and its dependency #1 | One evidence item, no candidate
   125	    └─ Evidence → Link to #1 (Party statement: NVIDIA says the US government assured it that H20 export licences to China will be granted (July 14, 2025))
   126	```
   127	
   128	Each evidence item records upstream ID, paragraph and exact quotation. Code checks that “$5.5 billion” appears in the quoted sentence. The first item sees an empty library, so important questions become candidates. The second sees the licence thesis and links NVIDIA's statement directly. The library compares candidates with searched theses and evidence within its thesis. Both candidates join the chip-licensing-and-China-business group; the evidence-only second item updates existing membership. Grouping does not replace #2's dependency on #1. The NVDA example uses the same process as equities, futures and cryptocurrencies.
   129	<!-- /generated: example -->
