   131	## Source-reference fields
   132	
   133	<!-- generated: sources -->
   134	```
   135	A source reference
   136	├─ Which item → Upstream ID (Each version has its own ID; use it to retrieve the original from the team's library)
   137	├─ Category and day → Category and publication day
   138	├─ Where in the item → Section, paragraph or table cell (For table data, record dataset and record IDs)
   139	└─ Which statement → The original sentence or table cell (Code verifies the quote exists in the item and the cited number appears there)
   140	```
   141	
   142	- Every thesis, evidence item and information-triggered change retains sources. Each state change identifies its supporting evidence, plus dependency theses when their changes triggered rejudgement.
   143	- Extraction checks quote/cell presence, number presence and links to block-5 theses or current candidates. Reject output failing any check. The library refuses sources absent from processing records. Both record reasons.
   144	- Trace in both directions: thesis to all source items, and item to every thesis and evidence item it created or changed.
   145	- Do not store the whole source item; retrieve it from the team's library by upstream ID. Store only the quoted sentence or cell supporting the thesis or evidence.
   146	- Repeated statements of the same fact within one item, such as a number in text and a table, become one evidence item with multiple references.
