   308	
   309	## Ordering, concurrency and traceability
   310	
   311	<!-- generated: order -->
   312	- Only incoming information creates evidence. Theses send notifications, not evidence, so they cannot prove themselves.
   313	- One queue writes each thesis: the theme's queue for theme theses; the company, cryptocurrency or commodity queue for asset theses. Within an item, write themes before assets. Rejudgement order follows dependencies under the round rule. Other queues send requests, such as dependency changes or replacement links, to the owning queue with the item's sequence number.
   314	- Order by sequence, never the computer clock. An item reads only records from earlier sequences plus its own round's writes. The result must match serial processing. If concurrent work changes anything it read, including search lists, before commit, redo the affected work.
   315	- One item's changes form a round. Finish evidence links, thesis creation, succession and dependencies, then rejudge in dependency order. Dependants read conclusions freshly judged in that round. Judge each thesis once. A widely depended-on thesis such as the Fed rate triggers each dependant once only if it declares its conclusion changed; otherwise propagation stops.
   316	- Append-only records: questions, ladders and due dates are immutable. Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labels append records with sequence numbers.
   317	- The timeline only appends, never rewrites past knowledge. A late statement already superseded by a newer one is logged as superseded and unused, not added to the thesis. Previously unknown information counts normally with its true date. The evidence-matching model distinguishes these cases; add no separate rule.
   318	- A revised input changes only its own source reference. Revising a retelling does not change original evidence. For an original-source revision, changed statements are replaced and deleted statements marked replaced. Rejudge each open thesis linked to affected statements once. A retelling-reference change alone does not trigger rejudgement. Keep old judgement records unchanged.
   319	- On failure, record the item's completed step and retry from the failed step, replacing only that attempt's own result. Repeated failures go to human review.
   320	- Trace both directions: a thesis to all information that affected it; an item to theses it created and all evidence it sourced, including repeats.
   321	
   322	Choose storage location and database during implementation.
   323	<!-- /generated: order -->
